A||||18||mshill||New Cat, melted in 1000 miles...||||8||
Z||000000||mshill||01-26-2000||01:13 AM||||I got a new cat back in October and only put about 1000 miles on my 86SE when on December 23rd the engine wouldn't develop any power above idle.  I noticed that there was little or no exhaust coming out of the tail pipes and my exhaust leak was  pushing out significant exhaust.  I thought that since I had a new cat, the muffler was plugged, but to the contrary, the cat was half melted and plugged.<P>I have the engine out for a rebuild right now, but before it was pulled I had the following two problems:  1) Exhaust leak on the front (firewall) manifold, and 2) the EGR code would come on when cruising for 10-15 minutes above 70 MPH.  The compression on all cylinders was 140-150 psi and I got between 19 and 22 MPG (half city, half highway).  Since the cat went on it has not overheated or got hot, and I was even running a 180 degree thermostat.<P>Could the exhaust leak have contributed to the early demise of the catalytic converter?<P>Any other ideas besides heat and rich/lean exhaust?<P>||||reg||8
Z||000001||mrfixit58||01-26-2000||08:32 AM||||Sounds like you've got a bad O2 sensor.||||reg||9
Z||000002||mshill||01-26-2000||10:56 AM||||O2 sensor was replaced 500 miles ago.  No reason to think it was bad, but I was just replacing everything to see if I could get the gas mileage up.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||01-26-2000||02:17 PM||||Did you do anything about the EGR code? I do not recall what function is affected by the signal of the EGR but that might be your lead. I doubt seriously that a leak in the manifold could affect the cat.||||reg||5
Z||000004||87PegasusGT||01-26-2000||03:32 PM||||I had the same problem. Turns out the shop put the cat on backwards. Go figure....||||reg||1
Z||000005||fierospeeder||01-26-2000||03:35 PM||||if your egr valve was stuck, you will release a lot of hydrocarbons.  Im not sure about how the ecm manages the mixture if the valve is bad.<BR>I think the only way to ruin a conv is to run rich. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||87PegasusGT||01-26-2000||03:52 PM||||Fiero cat only flows one way. You might want to check the arrows on your clogged one....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||mshill||01-26-2000||09:04 PM||||I was almost certain I put it in with the arrow going with the flow, cause I remember taking it apart once because I thought I had it backwards.  I could have put it back on the same way.<P>I was looking for the arrow, but couldn't find it when I got it off.  I will have to look again.  That would be a real stupid mistake if it is the case.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Gokart||01-26-2000||09:11 PM||||Where did you purchase the cat at? Please don't say Fiero Store cause I got mine through them and I got about 600 miles on it.||||reg||5
Z||000009||88formula||01-26-2000||10:20 PM||||A leak in the exhaust WILL affect the Catalytic converter. Here's how, air will enter the exhaust ahead of the O2 sensor through the exhaust leak. The O2 sensor will read the excess oxygen as a lean condition. The computer trying to compensate for the lean condition will richen the fuel mixture. The catalytic converter will overheat because your engine will be running rich as a result of the exhaust leak. That’s it, meltdown, simple. ASE-A1 THROU A8. Nothing wrong with the O2 sensor, Cat, or EGR.<p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 01-26-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ed||01-26-2000||10:24 PM||||I got one from Midas which lasted about a year and 10,000 miles. The replacement has been in over three years now. Your problem may have just been a bad "cat." <P>One other alternative as mentioned above is a poorly running engine fouling the "cat."||||reg||1
Z||000011||mshill||01-27-2000||12:32 AM||||It was on correctly.  I will have to check the EGR valve.  Funny thing is that without the cat (previous one was completely gone) it only failed smog testing marginally, so I wouldn't think that it would destroy a new cat so quickly.<P>The good thing is that I have the engine down and the exhaust manifolds have been rewelded and ground out, so hopefully the problem is done with.  Since I just got it smogged and I won't have to do it again for two years, I am just going to break out the bad cat and buy another one when I need it.<P>[This message has been edited by mshill (edited 01-27-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by mshill (edited 01-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||cobra427||01-27-2000||01:37 AM||||Have you used any type of spray cleaner like  Gumout or similar? Not all are cat friendly. just a thought.||||reg||1
Z||000013||WKDFIRO||01-27-2000||01:46 AM||||A leaking exhaust manifold would not necessarily be SUCKING in air would it? It is shooting exhaust OUT. Vacuum leaks create this situation but we have not seen a cat go south because of a leaking manifold in only 1000 miles. And we have replaced manifolds cracked all the way around. They melt plug wires, wire harnesses etc. but we have not had a bad manifold relate to bad cat problems so severe as described.<P>As for the welded manifolds I wish you luck. We have not been able to have a welded manifold sucessfully last for longer than a year before cracking at the weld.||||reg||5
Z||000014||Standard||01-27-2000||02:13 AM||||I was thinking about that leaking exhaust - sucking air problem for a bit, but there's no way (so far as i know) that a crack in the manifold will pull air in, unless you have no backpressure.  ||||reg||1
Z||000015||JIM GOOSE||01-27-2000||09:33 AM||||Whenever I have seen the RED HOT converter condition, it has been due to an over-rich exhaust. This was caused by timing off and /or misfiring of plugs. When the rich mix hits the converter, it lights up like a blast furnace.||||reg||1
Z||000016||862M4inCA||01-27-2000||06:39 PM||||Have you noticed the engine burning oil? I had to replace a cat due to oil bypass which caused the cat to plug up. Also in response to the exhaust leak question, as I recall from my auto courses that there are times at which the exhaust manifold does experience a "below atmospheric pressure". I believe that it occures when the exhaust valve is closing and the piston is begining it's intake stroke. Anyone else know anything about this?||||reg||1
Z||000017||mshill||01-27-2000||06:55 PM||||I have a hard time believing that the condition was caused by a poor running engine.  The plugs showed no signs of being fouled or too rich a mixture.  My gas mileage was between 19 and 22 MPG just before the problem ocurred.  The engine didn't appear to be burning excessive amounts of oil either.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||mshill||01-27-2000||06:58 PM||||cobra427,  I did recently use a Fuel System cleaner (injector/combustion chamber cleaner) that you put in your gas tank.  It said it would not harm the cat but I wonder now if it didn't have some affect.||||reg||1
